# Labkoat-API environment overrides.
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and edit. `dotenv/config` is imported as the first line of
# src/api-server.js, so these are read before src/config.js resolves anything.
#
# Only settings you want to vary per machine belong here. Endpoints and non-secret IDs live in
# src/config.js; secrets come from AWS Secrets Manager, and AWS credentials from the default
# provider chain (~/.aws/credentials locally, the pod's IAM role in the mesh) — never from here.


# --- Pipeline storage -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where uploads for /api/pipeline/v1 and /api/ingest/v1 are written.
#
#   local   a directory on this machine. No S3 bucket, IAM role or project storage record needed,
#           so the whole upload -> run -> poll path works out of the box. References become
#           file://... instead of s3://... , which the Portal neither sees nor cares about.
#   s3      each project's own bucket, read from `storage.bucket` on its fMam record. Set that on
#           the Admin page. Credentials come from the default AWS provider chain.
#
# Defaults to `local` when started with `env=local`, and to `s3` otherwise — so you only need this
# line to override that, e.g. to test a real bucket from your machine.
#PIPELINE_STORAGE=s3

# Directory `local` storage uses, resolved against the Labkoat-API folder (not the working
# directory, so an IDE run configuration cannot move it). Defaults to .pipeline-local
#PIPELINE_LOCAL_ROOT=.pipeline-local

# Master bucket for pipeline source files, subdivided <project>/<pipelineId>/<filename>.
# Defaults to labkoat-project.
#PIPELINE_BUCKET=labkoat-project


# --- Pipeline credentials ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT set here. A pipeline declares the credentials it needs by name (`secrets: ['yamdu']`), and
# src/pipeline/secrets.js maps each name to a key in the AWS Secrets Manager entry read at boot —
# for Yamdu, LABKOAT.YAMDU_API_TOKEN. A run is handed only what its own pipeline declared.
#
# Credentials are never stored on a project record: that would put them in Mongo and send them to
# the browser with the project list. Per-project values that are NOT secret — a Yamdu project id,
# say — go in the project's key/value settings on the Admin page instead.
#
# Not every credential is the service's to hold. A pipeline reading an API that authenticates the
# *user* — Frame.io, via Adobe IMS — has no service token at all: the browser logs the user in with
# PKCE and sends the resulting access token with the run request, and secrets.js takes that name
# from the request rather than from AWS. Nothing about it is configured here, and nothing is
# persisted. See USER_SUPPLIED in src/pipeline/secrets.js and the Portal's Pipeline/CONTRACTS.md.
